'''Charlie Robinson''' (November 9, 1945 – July 11, 2021) was an American stage, film and television actor. He is best known for his role on the [[NBC]] sitcom ''[[Night Court]]'' as Macintosh "Mac" Robinson (Seasons 2–9), the clerk of the court and a [[Vietnam War]] veteran.

Although his most frequent on-screen billing was Charlie Robinson, ''Night Court'' had credited him as Charles Robinson throughout his 1984–1992 stint as Mac. In two of his earliest film appearances, 1974's ''[[Sugar Hill (1974 film)|Sugar Hill]]'' and 1975's ''[[The Black Gestapo]]'', he was credited as Charles P. Robinson. Some of his credits have been occasionally commingled with those of older actor [[Charles Knox Robinson]] who, between 1958 and 1971, was featured in numerous films and television episodes under the stage name Charles Robinson.

==Early life==
Robinson was born in [[Houston]], to Planey and Ora (Barnes) Robinson. He served in the Army and briefly attended the [[University of Houston]] before his departure to pursue an acting career.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Grullón Paz |first=Isabella |date=July 13, 2021 |title=Charlie Robinson, Actor Best Known for 'Night Court,' Dies at 75 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/07/13/arts/television/charlie-robinson-dead.html |access-date=January 25, 2024 |website=[[New York Times]]}}</ref>

==Early career==
In Robinson's early career, he was a singer: as a teenager with the group [[Archie Bell and the Drells]] and later with a group called Southern Clouds of Joy.<ref>{{cite news| url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=Uw4wAAAAIBAJ&sjid=8AMEAAAAIBAJ&pg=6897,2046393&dq=archie+bell+and+the+drells+robinson&hl=en| title=Celebrity Mailbag| newspaper=[[The Blade (Toledo, Ohio)|The Blade]]| location=Toledo, Ohio| date=1998-11-05| accessdate=2012-12-08}}</ref>

==Later career==
Robinson's early acting credits include appearances in the films ''[[Sugar Hill (1974 film)|Sugar Hill]]'' (1974), ''[[Black Gestapo]]'' (1975), and the television series ''[[The White Shadow (TV series)|The White Shadow]]'', ''[[Lou Grant (TV series)|Lou Grant]]'', ''[[Flamingo Road (TV series)|Flamingo Road]]'', ''[[Hill Street Blues]]'', and ''[[St. Elsewhere]]''.  Robinson was cast in the role of Newdell in the [[NBC]] comedy ''[[Buffalo Bill (TV series)|Buffalo Bill]]''.  Not the success it was expected to be, ''Buffalo Bill'' was canceled after two seasons and replaced by ''[[Night Court]]''. Robinson was cast as court clerk Mac Robinson, after the first season in 1984, when [[Karen Austin]], who played the original court clerk, left the cast.  Robinson played the role on ''Night Court'' from 1984 until the show ended in 1992. He also directed three episodes of the series.

From 1992 to 1995, Robinson co-starred on the sitcom ''[[Love & War (TV series)|Love & War]]'', replacing [[John Hancock (actor)|John Hancock]] who died a few episodes into the series run.<ref>{{cite book| title=The Complete Directory to Prime Time Network and Cable TV Shows, 1946-Present| url=https://books.google.com/books?id=w8KztFy6QYwC&q=Love+%26+war| first1=Tim| last1=Brooks| first2=Earle F.| last2=Marsh| publisher=Ballantine Books| location=New York| page=815| date=June 24, 2009| isbn=978-0-3074-8320-1| access-date=April 1, 2023}}</ref>  Robinson played character Bud Harper in ''[[Home Improvement (TV series)|Home Improvement]]'', and appeared in many other television shows including ''[[The Fresh Prince of Bel Air]]'', ''[[Touched by an Angel]]'', ''[[Soul Food (TV series)|Soul Food]]'', ''[[Antwone Fisher (film)|Antwone Fisher]]'', ''[[My Wife and Kids]]'', ''[[The Bernie Mac Show]]'', ''[[Charmed]]'', ''[[House (TV series)|House]]'', ''[[Cold Case]]'', ''[[How I Met Your Mother]]'', ''[[My Name Is Earl]]'', ''[[The Game (U.S. TV series)|The Game]]'', and ''[[Hart of Dixie]]''.<ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/hart-dixie-season-2-spoilers-charlie-robinson-356557| last=Goldberg| first=Lesley| title='Hart of Dixie' Taps 'Night Court' Funnyman for Recurring Gig (Exclusive)| newspaper=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]| date=July 31, 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.tvguide.com/news/hart-dixie-charlie-robinson-golden-brooks-1051148/| last=Gennis| first=Sadie| title='Hart of Dixie' Books ''Night Court's'' Charlie Robinson| magazine=[[TV Guide]]| date=August 1, 2012}}</ref>

He did commercial work for [[NEXTEL]] in which he asks a worker if he's "agitating my dots" after he walks in on two other dispatchers staring at the dots, which represented delivery workers, on a computer screen. He also did commercials for [[Old Spice]], where he played the head coach of the [[NFL]]'s [[Denver Broncos]],  appearing with perennial All-Pro Bronco [[linebacker]] [[Von Miller]].

In 2010, Robinson worked at the Oregon Shakespeare Festival and co-starred in the film ''[[Jackson (2008 film)|Jackson]]'' which was directed by [[J. F. Lawton]]. Robinson appeared as "Troy" in [[August Wilson]]'s ''[[Fences (play)|Fences]]'' at [[Southern California]]'s [[South Coast Repertory]] in [[Costa Mesa]] from January 22, 2010 until February 21, 2010.<ref>{{cite web| title='Fences' program| url=https://www.scr.org/media/hn3ds1fi/fencesprog.pdf| date=January–February 2010| website=South Coast Repertory| access-date=April 1, 2023}}</ref> In September 2013, he returned to the theater to portray [[Willy Loman]] in ''[[Death of a Salesman]]''.<ref>{{cite news| url=http://thisstage.la/2013/09/charlie-robinson-and-scrs-masterson-pay-attention-to-willy-loman/| last=Donloe| first=Darlene| title=Charlie Robinson and SCR's Masterson Pay Attention to Willy Loman| magazine=This Stage Magazine| date=September 6, 2013| access-date=January 8, 2017| archive-date=January 9, 2017| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170109183953/http://thisstage.la/2013/09/charlie-robinson-and-scrs-masterson-pay-attention-to-willy-loman/| url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.latimes.com/entertainment/arts/culture/la-et-cm-charlie-robinson-death-salesman-20130908-story.html| last=Boehm| first=Mike| title=Charlie Robinson has no delusions playing Willy Loman| newspaper=[[Los Angeles Times]]| date=September 7, 2013}}</ref> In 2015, he played Mr. Munson, the blind tenant on ''[[Mom (TV series)|Mom]]'' whom Bonnie avoids helping with apartment issues.

==Illness and death==
Robinson died on July 11, 2021, at [[Ronald Reagan UCLA Medical Center]], from cardiac arrest with [[multisystem organ failure]] due to [[septic shock]] and metastatic [[adenocarcinoma]]. He was 75.<ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/charlie-robinson-night-court-star-dead-1234980994/| title=Charlie Robinson, 'Night Court' Star, Dies at 75| first=Lexy| last=Perez| magazine=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]| date=July 12, 2021| access-date=July 13, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.rollingstone.com/tv/tv-news/charlie-robinson-actor-dead-obituary-1196400/| title=Charlie Robinson, 'Night Court' Actor, Dead at 75| first=Althea| last=Legaspi| magazine=[[Rolling Stone]]| date=July 12, 2021| access-date=July 13, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news| url=https://deadline.com/2021/07/charles-robinson-dead-night-court-buffalo-bill-1234791266/| title=Charlie Robinson Dies: 'Night Court' & 'Buffalo Bill' Actor Was 75| first=Erik| last=Pedersen| website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]| date=July 12, 2021| access-date=July 13, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news| url=https://variety.com/2021/tv/news/charlie-robinson-dead-mac-night-court-1235018057/| title=Charlie Robinson, Who Played Mac on 'Night Court,' Dies at 75| first=Ethan| last=Shanfeld| magazine=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]| date=July 12, 2021| access-date=July 13, 2021}}</ref>

